  "notes": "West leads the ♣4 against 6♦. As South does not need an extra club trick, but may well need extra dummy entries, he should win the ♣K at trick 1. The only relevant problem occurs when West has all 4 diamonds.\n\nAfter cashing the ♥A South should lead a middle diamond. If West plays low South should win the ♦A as the odds of being able to handle West having 4 diamonds are not as high as the odds that East could win and give West a ruff.\n\nWest actually covers and North wins the ♦A as the 4-0 trump break is revealed. South must use this dummy entry and hope for 2 more entries in clubs to reduce his trump length to that of West. South will have to guess West's distribution.\n\nFor the trump coup to work West must have exactly 3 clubs to go with his known 4 diamonds. As East rates to be longer than West in both spades and hearts, West should be played for 3343 distribution.\n\nA heart is played from dummy and ruffed by South. The ♠A and ♠K are cashed and dummy is entered with a club finesse. A spade is ruffed. South crosses again in clubs and plays a heart, ruffing again.\n\nSouth and West are both down to only 3 diamonds. South can exit a middle diamond and take 2 of the last 3 tricks.",
